**Theorem 5.2**.: _We retain all the hypotheses of Lemma 5.1 except (5.17). Then the solution \(u_{h}=(\mathbf{curl}\,\psi_{h},\omega_{h})\) of Problem_ (Q\({}_{h}\)) _satisfies the error estimate_:__

(5.21) \[\|\omega-\omega_{h}\|_{\,0,\,\Omega}\leq 2\inf_{v_{h}\in V_{h}}|u-v_{h}| +(1+C^{*2})^{1/2}\inf_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{h}\in M_{h}}\|\omega-\boldsymbol{\mu}_ {h}\|_{H(\mathbf{curl},\,\Omega)}\] \[\|\mathbf{curl}(\psi-\psi_{h})\|_{\,0,\,\Omega}\leq(1+C^{*2})^{1/2}\] (5.22) \[\qquad\times\biggl{\{}\inf_{v_{h}\in V_{h}}\|u-v_{h}\|_{\,\vec{ \boldsymbol{x}}}+C^{*}\inf_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{h}\in M_{h}}\|\omega-\boldsymbol {\mu}_{h}\|_{H(\mathbf{curl},\,\Omega)}\biggr{\}},\]

_where C* is the constant of (5.14)._

Like in the two-dimensional case, we are now faced with the evaluation of the approximation error of \(V_{h}\): \(\inf_{v_{h}\in V_{h}}\|u-v_{h}\|_{\,\vec{\boldsymbol{x}}}\). It is easy to see that the statement of Lemma 3.1 is still valid here.

**Lemma 5.2**.: _With the notations of Lemma 5.1 we have the upper bound for all \(v=(\mathbf{curl}\,\phi,\theta)\in\vec{V}\):_

(5.23) \[\inf_{\boldsymbol{w}_{h}\in V_{h}}|v-w_{h}|\leq\inf_{v_{h}=(\mathbf{curl}\, \phi_{h},\,\theta_{h})\in X_{h}}\biggl{\{}2|v-v_{h}|+\sup_{\boldsymbol{\mu}_{ h}\in M_{h}}\frac{(\mathbf{curl}(\phi-\phi_{h}),\mathbf{curl}\,\boldsymbol{\mu}_ {h})}{\|\boldsymbol{\mu}_{h}\|_{\,0,\,\Omega}}\biggr{\}},\]

_and a similar upper bound for \(\|v-w_{h}\|_{\,\vec{\boldsymbol{x}}}\) with the norm \(|.|\) replaced by \(\|\,.\,\|_{\,\vec{\boldsymbol{x}}}\) in the right-hand side side of (5.23)._

### A Family of Conforming Finite Elements in \(H(\mathbf{curl};\Omega)\)

In this section, we present a space of finite elements developed by Nedelec [59; 60]. Its construction is by no means straightforward, inasmuch as it requires exactly the continuity of the tangential components at element interfaces. This implies that we must work with incomplete spaces of polynomials of (say) degree \(l\), for some integer \(l\geq 1\).

Let \(\bar{P}_{l}\) denote the space of homogeneous polynomials of degree \(l\) in \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\) and consider the following subspaces of \(P_{l}^{3}\):

(5.24) \[\begin{cases}S_{l}=\{\mathbf{p}\in\bar{P}_{l}^{3};\,\mathbf{p}(x)\cdot\mathbf{ x}\equiv 0,\,\mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3})\},\\ \qquad\qquad R_{l}=P_{l-1}^{3}\oplus S_{l}.\end{cases}\]

_Examples._ Let us exhibit \(S_{1}\) and \(S_{2}\). Clearly, all homogeneous polynomial vectors of degree one that satisfy \(\mathbf{p}(x)\cdot\mathbf{x}=0\) must necessarily be of the form:

\[\mathbf{p}(x)=\boldsymbol{\alpha}\times\mathbf{x}\]

where \(\boldsymbol{\alpha}\) is an arbitrary vector of \(\mathbb{R}^{3}\). Thus, \(S_{1}\) has the basis:

\[\mathbf{p}_{1}(x)=(0,-x_{3},x_{2}),\quad\quad\mathbf{p}_{2}(x)=(\mathbf{x}_{3 },0,-x_{1}),\]

\[\mathbf{p}_{3}(x)=(-x_{2},x_{1},0).\]
